 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The visibility calculation is efficient due to the use of 2D boundary tracing on slices, which is faster than full 3D ray casting for each voxel [S1, unofficial]. The complexity is roughly proportional to the number of voxels and the number of viewing directions.
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing a list of boundary voxels, which is significantly smaller than the full volume data [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Fast rendering speed, simple implementation, easy integration of cutting planes and lighting [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Approximate visibility (only six orthographic views may miss some surfaces), potential for holes if projection is not handled carefully (mitigated by projecting to multiple pixels) [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** Fast previewing, medical imaging systems where cutting planes are important, scenes with simple geometry [S1, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** High-quality final rendering where occlusion is complex, scenes with many internal surfaces [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the visibility calculation. For $N$ viewing directions and $V$ voxels, the cost is roughly $O(N \cdot V)$ for boundary tracing, plus $O(V)$ for culling and projection [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ to store the list of visible voxels and their attributes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast rendering speed due to reduced number of primitives; simple implementation; supports cutting planes easily [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Approximate visibility (depends on number of views); potential for holes if multi-pixel projection is not used; less geometric accuracy than polygon-based methods [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Real-time previewing, medical imaging systems requiring fast manipulation, scenes with large volumes where full raycasting is too slow [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** High-fidelity rendering requiring precise geometry, scenes with complex occlusion not covered by the chosen viewing directions [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Proposed by Sobierajski [116] as a fast display method for direct rendering of boundary surfaces [S1, unofficial]. It is cited in the context of medical imaging systems where cutting operations are important [S1, unofficial]. The method is part of a broader class of hybrid acceleration methods, though it is distinct from shear-warp factorization [S1, unofficial].
+The method was proposed by Sobierajski [116] as a fast display method for direct rendering of boundary surfaces [S1, unofficial]. It is part of the broader field of volume rendering techniques, specifically focusing on surface extraction and acceleration [S1, unofficial]. The approach is often compared to Saito's Fast Previewing Algorithm, which uses a similar principle but with a subset of voxels [S1, unofficial].
 
